## Source and Evidence Audit

The sole admitted source is Dave Kuszmar's first-person account published in IEEE Spectrum on 2026-07-14 (https://spectrum.ieee.org/jailbreaking-llms). The research packet's own warning notes no source in this packet has been established as a primary record; this is a secondary publication of a researcher's self-report. Within those limits, attribution is consistent and named throughout the manuscript â€” "Kuszmar writes," "he describes," "Kuszmar reports" â€” and all factual claims trace directly to the admitted source text. The independent replication by Bleeping Computer EIC Lawrence Abrams and the SEI CERT disclosure are accurately characterized. No facts are invented.

## Material Factual Error: Count of AI Companies

The lede states that Kuszmar's second exploit, Inception, succeeded against "systems from OpenAI, Google, Anthropic, and **six other** commercial AI developers." The source names exactly eight companies: Anthropic, DeepSeek, Google, Meta, Microsoft, Mistral, OpenAI, and xAI. Naming three explicitly and appending "six other" produces a total of nine â€” one more than the source establishes. The correct phrasing is "five other." This is a material error because the scope of the vulnerability is the claim's point. Section 3 of the body correctly lists all eight companies individually and is unaffected by this repair.

## Institutional Mischaracterization: SEI CERT

The draft states that SEI CERT "operates a coordinating center that routes vulnerability disclosures to the U.S. Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency." The source states SEI CERT "works in conjunction with the coordinating center for emergency response, pipelining vulnerabilities to the U.S. Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency." The draft incorrectly attributes operational ownership of the coordinating center to SEI CERT; the source describes a collaborative relationship with an external entity. This is a structural attribution error. A bounded repair restores the correct relationship.

## Editorial Surfaces: Summary Truncation and Description Scope

The frontmatter summary is visibly truncated, ending mid-citation with "(spectrum. ieee." as a bare fragment. It also repeats the six-other count error. The description â€” "The system consistently anchored its understanding of events to its training cutoff rather than to the actual date of the conversation" â€” isolates one technical mechanism (the training-cutoff confusion underlying Time Bandit) rather than characterizing the article's overall subject: a researcher documenting systemic jailbreak vulnerabilities across the commercial LLM industry. Both surfaces require regeneration via repair_editorial_surfaces.

## Headline-Lede Alignment (Warning, Not Blocking)

The headline leads with "Vader's Guidance" â€” a reference to the Fortnite/Darth Vader anecdote that appears late in Section 3 as one of several illustrative examples. The lede organizes the story around systemic research findings and disclosure failures, not around the Vader episode. "How AI's Dark Side Was Unlocked" (the headline's second clause) is faithful; the leading Vader hook over-weights a supporting detail relative to the article's argument. This is a meaningful framing concern but not a factual falsehood, and the headline is frontmatter that repair_editorial_surfaces can revisit in generating updated surfaces.

## Citation Format (Production Note, Not Blocking)

The first paragraph contains factual claims before any hyperlinked URL citation appears. The story is named in prose as "*IEEE Spectrum*" with date but without a hyperlink; the first URL appears at the end of paragraph 2. Subsequent citations use parenthetical "(spectrum.ieee.org)" domain links rather than exact-title links. Attribution is consistent and named throughout, so this is a production-format concern rather than a truth-accuracy failure. It does not require a body text repair beyond what repair_editorial_surfaces may address in generated surfaces.

## Fairness Assessment

The manuscript criticizes AI companies â€” primarily OpenAI â€” for unresponsiveness to vulnerability disclosure. These characterizations are consistently attributed to Kuszmar's account rather than presented as newsroom findings. The draft does represent some company engagement: it notes OpenAI acknowledged the vulnerability during SEI CERT disclosure and expressed confusion about its mechanism. No independent response from criticized companies appears in the evidence base, which is a limitation of reporting from a single first-person source rather than an editorial failure of this manuscript.

## Deterministic Standards

Two warnings remain: `demand-lens-missing-first-paragraph` and `demand-lens-missing-h2`. The demand lens ("turned dark side") is naturally expressed in the headline and article frame; forcing exact wording into the first paragraph or a subheading would be artificial given the reporting. These are advisory, not blocking.

## Pipeline Diagnosis

All three defects are products of the writer model draft, not of thin sources or an unsupported assignment. The source evidence is adequate for the article's scope; the assignment is well-matched to the material. The failures are local: one arithmetic error in the lede, one ownership-versus-collaboration conflation in Section 2, and two malformed editorial surfaces. Bounded repairs at the writer-model layer are sufficient to make this manuscript publishable without a full replay.
